    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Living in a high-humidity environment, whether that is a tropical climate, a coastal city or a basement flat, brings its own interior design challenges. Humidity isn&rsquo;t just about personal discomfort; damp air settles into organic materials and encourages mould and mildew. Mould spoils the look of your home and affects the air you breathe, and damp and mould can affect health, particularly for people with breathing conditions or allergies &mdash; seek professional advice for persistent mould.</p>
<p>The secret to a mould-free home in a humid climate lies largely in the materials you choose. Traditional favourites such as untreated wood, natural cotton and paper-based wallpapers give mould something to feed on. To create a stylish, breathable space that lasts, lean towards non-porous, inorganic and moisture-resistant elements. If you have been searching for &ldquo;decor that doesn&rsquo;t mold in a humid apartment&rdquo;, this guide explores the choices that cope best with damp.</p>

<h2 id="decor-or-damp">First, check whether it is decor or damp</h2>
<p>Decor can only do so much. Black spotting on walls, ceilings or window reveals, a persistent musty smell, peeling paint or tide marks usually point to a moisture problem in the building: condensation from poor ventilation, a leak, or rising or penetrating damp. If you rent, report mould on walls to your landlord or managing agent in writing, with dated photos, rather than painting over it. Covering it with furniture or a fresh coat of paint hides the symptom and can make the problem worse. Once the cause is dealt with, the choices below help the room stay fresh.</p>

<h2 id="metal-accents">1. Metal accents</h2>
<!--figure:dining-room-sculptural-mirrors|Sculptural metal mirrors and a brass pendant: metal accents cope well with humid air.-->
<p>Metal is one of the most resilient materials in humid air. Unlike wood, it is non-porous, so it does not absorb moisture, and mould has very little to take hold of beyond surface dust. From industrial-style shelving to elegant gold-rimmed mirrors, metal adds a touch of sophistication without the risk of rot.</p>
<p>When choosing metal decor, look for rust-resistant materials. Stainless steel, aluminium and powder-coated metals are ideal. Brass and copper also work well, though they may develop a patina over time in humid air, which many people find attractive. Avoid cheap plated metals that might flake, as moisture can get trapped under the plating and cause corrosion.</p>

<h2 id="glass-elements">2. Glass elements</h2>
<p>Glass is one of the cleanest materials for a humid flat. It is inorganic and non-porous, and mould only grows on it when dust and grime build up on the surface. Glass coffee tables, side tables and decorative partitions help a room feel light and airy, which many people find welcome in heavy, humid weather.</p>
<p>Glass also reflects light. In many humid climates, flats can feel dark or heavy, and mirrors and glass surfaces bounce natural light around the room, making it feel fresher and more spacious. Cleaning is as simple as a quick wipe with a microfibre cloth and diluted vinegar.</p>

<h2 id="glazed-ceramics">3. Glazed ceramics</h2>
<p>While raw terracotta can absorb moisture, glazed ceramics are a humid flat&rsquo;s best friend. The glaze creates a glass-like barrier over the porous clay, making the piece water-resistant and easy to keep clean. Ceramics are perfect for plant pots, decorative bowls and even wall art.</p>
<p>In a humid home, glazed ceramic pots are a better choice than unglazed clay pots for indoor plants. Unglazed clay can &ldquo;sweat&rdquo; moisture onto furniture and develop white mould or mineral deposits on the outside. Glazed versions keep the moisture in the soil, protecting your surfaces and keeping the decor looking smart.</p>

<h2 id="synthetic-textiles">4. Synthetic textiles</h2>
<p>Natural fibres such as cotton, linen and wool absorb moisture. In a humid flat they can hold onto damp and develop mildew, and if you have ever noticed a musty smell on your sofa, damp fabric is the likely cause.</p>
<p>One solution is high-quality synthetic textiles. Modern polyester, nylon and acrylic fabrics have come a long way: they can mimic the feel of silk or linen while shedding water and drying quickly. For rugs, look for outdoor-rated polypropylene rugs, which are stylish, durable and highly resistant to mould (mould can still grow on dirt and dust, so clean them regularly). If you prefer natural fibres, choose removable, washable covers and throws so you can launder them whenever they start to smell stale.</p>

<h2 id="teak-furniture">5. Teak furniture</h2>
<p>If you love the look of wood, you don&rsquo;t have to give it up. You just need to choose the right species and finish. Teak is well known for its moisture resistance: its natural oils act as a built-in water repellent, which is why it is a long-standing favourite for boat decks and outdoor furniture.</p>
<p>In a humid flat, teak is far less likely to warp, crack or mould than pine or cheaper composite boards, which can swell at the edges when moisture gets in. It is an investment, but it lasts well in damp conditions. Other moisture-resistant woods include cedar and ipe, though teak remains the most common for refined indoor furniture. Whatever the wood, a good lacquer, oil or varnish matters; re-oil oiled pieces as the maker recommends.</p>

<h2 id="polished-stone">6. Polished stone</h2>
<p>Natural stone such as marble and granite, and engineered quartz, brings an earthy, grounded feel to a flat. When properly sealed, these surfaces resist humidity well. They are also cool to the touch, which can make a room feel fresher in a hot, humid climate.</p>
<p>Stone tabletops, coasters and decorative trays are a smart choice. Avoid porous stones such as unfinished limestone or sandstone, which can trap moisture in their pores. Stick to sealed, polished surfaces that let moisture sit on top rather than soak in, so you can wipe it away easily.</p>

<h2 id="acrylic-furniture">7. Acrylic furniture</h2>
<p>Acrylic is a high-grade plastic with the visual clarity of glass, but it is lighter and less likely to shatter. Because it does not absorb moisture, it is highly resistant to mould (mould can still grow on dirt and dust, so clean it regularly).</p>
<p>Acrylic &ldquo;ghost&rdquo; chairs and clear shelving are fantastic for small, humid flats. They take up little visual space, which helps a room feel uncluttered, and a less crowded room is easier to keep aired. They are modern and chic, and easy to clean with a soft cloth and mild soapy water (check the manufacturer&rsquo;s care advice, as some cleaners can cloud acrylic).</p>

<h2 id="high-quality-vinyl">8. High-quality vinyl</h2>
<p>Traditional paper wallpaper struggles in humid climates: the adhesive can fail, and the paper itself gives mould something to feed on. Modern vinyl wallcoverings are moisture-resistant and can be wiped or scrubbed clean, so they are a much better fit for damp rooms.</p>
<p>Vinyl flooring is another good choice. Modern luxury vinyl plank (LVP) can look very like hardwood but is waterproof, so it won&rsquo;t swell like laminate or rot like real wood. If you are decorating walls or floors in a damp flat, vinyl offers a good balance of durability and choice. Renters should check their tenancy agreement and get the landlord&rsquo;s written permission before changing floors or wallcoverings.</p>

<h2 id="specialised-paints">9. Specialised paints</h2>
<p>Sometimes the decor is the wall itself. In humid flats, the type of paint you choose matters. Standard flat matt emulsion is harder to wipe clean, while mould-resistant or &ldquo;anti-microbial&rdquo; paints are formulated to discourage mould on the painted surface.</p>
<p>Mould-resistant paints can help on surfaces, but they don&rsquo;t fix the cause &mdash; ventilation and dealing with damp do. Satin, eggshell and semi-gloss finishes are generally easier to wipe down than flat matt in kitchens and bathrooms. Never paint over existing mould; deal with the source first and follow the paint manufacturer&rsquo;s preparation guidance.</p>

<h2 id="plants-carefully">10. Plants, carefully</h2>
<p>Houseplants release moisture into the air through their leaves and soil, so in a humid flat it is best to keep just a few rather than a jungle. Choose a handful of healthy plants and place them where air moves freely.</p>
<p>The key is the soil and the pot. Use well-draining compost and pots with drainage holes, set on saucers that you empty regularly, so water never sits and stagnates. Popular choices such as snake plants and peace lilies are toxic to cats and dogs, so if you have pets, check each species first; our guide to <a href="/blog/non-toxic-decor-for-cats">non-toxic decor for cats</a> covers safer options.</p>

<h2 id="airflow-and-dehumidifying">Airflow, dehumidifiers and layout</h2>
<p>Most mould problems in furniture and fabric come down to still, damp air. Small habits make a big difference.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keep air moving:</strong> Open trickle vents if your windows have them, use extractor fans while cooking and showering and for a while afterwards, and open windows briefly each day where it is safe to do so.</li>
<li><strong>Keep furniture off cold external walls:</strong> Outside walls are where condensation forms. Leave a gap of at least 5&ndash;10 cm (2&ndash;4 in) behind wardrobes, sofas and bookcases so air can circulate.</li>
<li><strong>Raise furniture:</strong> Pieces on legs of 10&ndash;15 cm (4&ndash;6 in) let air flow underneath and are easier to keep fresh than solid plinth bases.</li>
<li><strong>Use a dehumidifier:</strong> In a persistently damp flat, a dehumidifier often does more than any decorating choice. Follow the manufacturer&rsquo;s guidance on placement, emptying and filter cleaning, and do the same for any fans.</li>
<li><strong>Dry laundry outside or near ventilation:</strong> Drying clothes on airers in a closed room adds a lot of moisture to the air.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Before buying a sofa or headboard for a damp home, check the care label for removable, washable covers. It is the single feature that will keep it fresh the longest.</p></blockquote>
<p>If heat rather than damp is your main problem, see our separate guide to <a href="/blog/decor-for-hot-climates-without-ac">decor for hot climates without AC</a>.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>A home that copes with humidity starts with dealing with the cause of damp, then choosing materials that give mould little to feed on: metal, glass, glazed ceramics, sealed stone, well-finished hardwoods and washable or synthetic fabrics. Add good airflow, a little space behind furniture and regular cleaning, and you can enjoy a stylish flat that stays fresh through the most humid months.</p>

<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3 id="mould-on-furniture-not-walls">Why does mould grow on my furniture but not the walls?</h3>
<p>Mould grows where it finds food and still air. Furniture made of organic materials such as wood, leather or natural fabric provides that food. If furniture is pushed against a wall, it creates a pocket of still air where moisture collects, so mould can appear on the back of a piece even when the wall looks clear. Check behind large pieces regularly, and if you find mould on the wall itself, report it to your landlord in writing.</p>
<h3 id="leather-in-humid-apartment">Can I use leather decor in a humid apartment?</h3>
<p>Leather is a natural, porous material that is prone to mould when relative humidity is consistently above about 60%. If you choose leather, keep it conditioned as the maker recommends and keep humidity down with ventilation, a dehumidifier or air conditioning. Faux leather is often a more practical alternative, as it is usually plastic-based, though it still needs wiping clean.</p>
<h3 id="protect-books-from-mould">How can I protect my books from mould?</h3>
<p>Books are made of paper and glue, which mould likes. Don&rsquo;t place bookshelves against cold outside walls, where condensation forms. Keep books in a room with good airflow, and consider a glass-fronted cabinet with silica gel sachets inside to absorb excess moisture, replacing or recharging them as directed.</p>
<h3 id="bamboo-in-humid-climates">Is bamboo a good choice for humid climates?</h3>
<p>Yes, if it is properly treated and sealed. Raw bamboo can mould if it stays damp, but solid or carbonised bamboo sealed with a water-resistant finish holds up well in humid conditions.</p>
<h3 id="air-conditioner-and-mould">Does an air conditioner help prevent mould on decor?</h3>
<p>Yes. Air conditioners remove moisture as they cool the air, which reduces the conditions mould needs. Many units have a &ldquo;dry&rdquo; mode for humid weather. If you don&rsquo;t have air conditioning, a dehumidifier does a similar job for moisture, and good ventilation helps either way.</p>

